    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Interest in the ability of fracture networks within geologic formations to transmit fluids has grown dramatically over the past decade; over three hundred references covering the three year period from 1987 to 1990 are cited by Wang(In Prep.). The endeavor understand the fundamentals of fracture flow has not been limited to one particular field. Natural fracture networks are known to play an important role in hydrocarbon production in as manyas two hundred oil and gas fields (Nelson 1985) and can influence the growth and final geometry of hydrofractures used to enhance production in scads of other fields (Lorenz, Warpinskiet al. 1988; Teufel and Clark 1984). Deloule and Turcotte (1989) have shown that fractures can important in the genesis of large Mississippi Valley- type ore deposits. The impact of fracture networks on gas migration above low- and medium-level radioactive waste repositories is a concern in Sweden (Thunvikand Braester 1990). While closer to home, the thermohydrologic behavior of the fractured tuff comprising Yucca Mountain has been a major factor in determining the Mountain’s suitability as a geologic repository for high-level nuclear waste(Pruess, Wang et al. 1990). Finally, the influence of fracture networks on groundwater flow and contaminant transport has recently received greater attention due to the increasingly common introduction of liquid toxic waste into tight formations (Cacas, Ledouxet al. 1990b; Dverstorp and Andersson1989; Moreno, Tsang et al. 1990; Smith and Schwartz 1984; Tsang and Tsang 1987).",
